{% sec 1, "Where the Return Comes From", "What's actually inside a long/short fund's return?", "Where the return comes from" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Imagine a sailing race on a windy day. Every boat goes faster with the wind behind it, so a fast time doesn't prove a good sailor. To judge the sailor, you'd want to know how much of the speed came from the wind, how much from a current that happened to flow the right way, and how much from the sailor's own skill.</p>
    <p>A long/short fund's return has the same kinds of parts:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Where it comes from</th><th>Could an investor get it cheaply elsewhere?</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>{% gl "beta" %}Beta{% endgl %} (the wind)</td><td>The fund's net exposure to the market. At \(40\%\) net long, it catches about \(40\%\) of the market's moves, up and down</td><td>Yes: an index fund</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Factor tilts (the current)</td><td>Leaning towards a style, such as growth, quality or momentum, that happened to do well</td><td>Mostly: factor ETFs</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Short rebate</td><td>Interest earned on the cash from short sales (the Short Selling note explains the {% gl "rebate rate" %}rebate{% endgl %})</td><td>Yes: cash in the bank</td></tr>
        <tr><td>{% gl "alpha" %}Alpha{% endgl %} (the sailor)</td><td>Picking stocks that beat the market and shorting ones that lag it</td><td>No: this is what hedge fund fees pay for</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The short rebate was almost nothing when interest rates sat near zero after 2008. With rates at \(4\)–\(5\%\), it's real money: a fund that's \(60\%\) short earning \(4\%\) on the cash gets about \(2.4\%\) a year without doing anything clever.</p>
    <p>An uncomfortable finding from research by AQR, among others, is that a large share of the average long/short fund's return can be copied with simple market and factor exposures. In other words, much of what the industry delivers on average is wind and current, not sailing. The best funds are the ones whose returns can't be copied that way.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Peel the return</h3><p>Start with the fund's total return for the year, then take away each part that isn't skill. Whatever is left is alpha. The defaults are Deltashark's example: \(12\%\) in a year the market rose \(20\%\), at \(40\%\) net long.</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="pr-tot">Fund return</label><input type="range" id="pr-tot" min="-10" max="30" step="1" value="12"><output id="pr-tot-out" for="pr-tot">12%</output></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="pr-mkt">Market return</label><input type="range" id="pr-mkt" min="-30" max="30" step="1" value="20"><output id="pr-mkt-out" for="pr-mkt">20%</output></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="pr-net">Net exposure</label><input type="range" id="pr-net" min="0" max="100" step="5" value="40"><output id="pr-net-out" for="pr-net">40%</output></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="pr-short">Short book</label><input type="range" id="pr-short" min="0" max="100" step="5" value="0"><output id="pr-short-out" for="pr-short">0%</output></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="pr-rate">Rebate rate</label><input type="range" id="pr-rate" min="0" max="6" step="0.5" value="4"><output id="pr-rate-out" for="pr-rate">4%</output></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="pr-fac">Factor tailwind</label><input type="range" id="pr-fac" min="-5" max="5" step="0.5" value="0"><output id="pr-fac-out" for="pr-fac">0%</output></div>
    </div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-pr" viewBox="0 0 640 260" role="img" aria-label="Waterfall from the fund's total return down to alpha, taking off market exposure, the short rebate and factor tilts"></svg>
    <div class="readout" id="pr-read"></div>
    <p class="look" id="pr-story"></p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "Alpha Separation", "How do allocators tell skill from luck and market exposure?", "Alpha separation" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>{% gl "alpha separation" %}Alpha separation{% endgl %} means splitting a fund's return into the part explained by the market (and factors) and the part that isn't. Allocators do it with a {% gl "regression" %}regression{% endgl %}: plot each month's fund return against the market's return for the same month, and fit a straight line through the dots.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The line's <strong>slope</strong> is the fund's beta: how much it moves for each \(1\%\) the market moves.</li>
      <li>Where the line <strong>crosses zero</strong> on the market axis (the intercept) is the fund's alpha: what it makes in a month when the market does nothing.</li>
      <li>How tightly the dots hug the line ({% gl "R-squared" %}R-squared{% endgl %}) says how much of the fund's ups and downs are just the market. High R-squared and low alpha is an expensive index fund.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Real allocators go further and add factors to the regression (value, momentum, quality, size), so a fund doesn't get credit for riding a style. But even the simple version reveals a lot, including how hard it is to be sure of alpha from a short record.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Skill or wind?</h3><p>Set a fund's true beta and true alpha, and how much luck there is each month. The chart draws three years of made-up monthly returns and fits a line, the way an allocator would. Compare what the line finds with the truth you set, then press "Run again".</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="sw-b">True beta</label><input type="range" id="sw-b" min="0" max="1.2" step="0.1" value="0.4"><output id="sw-b-out" for="sw-b">0.4</output></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="sw-a">True alpha</label><input type="range" id="sw-a" min="-4" max="8" step="1" value="4"><output id="sw-a-out" for="sw-a">4%/yr</output></div>
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="sw-n">Luck</label><input type="range" id="sw-n" min="0.5" max="4" step="0.5" value="2"><output id="sw-n-out" for="sw-n">2%/mo</output></div>
      <div class="presets ab-run"><button type="button" id="sw-again">Run again</button></div>
    </div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-sw" viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-label="Scatter of 36 monthly fund returns against market returns with a fitted line whose slope is beta and intercept is alpha"></svg>
    <div class="legend"><span><i class="sw tone"></i>Fitted line</span><span><i class="sw grey"></i>True line you set</span></div>
    <div class="readout" id="sw-read"></div>
    <p class="look" id="sw-story"></p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>Beta is cheap and easy to measure. Alpha is expensive and hard to prove: with a few years of noisy monthly returns, a skilled fund and a lucky one can look the same. That's why allocators want long track records, and why they keep checking after they invest.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "Is It Really Market-Neutral?", "How do you test a manager who says the market doesn't matter?", "Testing a neutral claim" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>"Market-neutral" is one of the most stretched phrases in the industry. A book can be neutral in three increasingly demanding ways: <strong>dollar-neutral</strong> (equal money long and short), <strong>beta-neutral</strong> (equal market sensitivity, the beta-adjusted net from the last note) and <strong>factor-neutral</strong> (no leftover style bets). Deltashark's list of questions to ask a manager who claims it:</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>What's your average net exposure?<span class="why">It should be near zero.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>What's your beta-adjusted net?<span class="why">Jumpy longs against steady shorts create hidden direction.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>What are your factor exposures?<span class="why">A dollar-neutral book can still be a big bet on value, momentum, size or a sector.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><span>How did you do in March 2020 and other sell-offs?<span class="why">A truly neutral fund shouldn't fall much when the market crashes.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">5</span><span>How much leverage do you use, and what happened when funds cut leverage together?<span class="why">Neutral books often borrow heavily, and suffer when everyone de-grosses at once.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">6</span><span>How much of your past return is explained by common factors?<span class="why">If most of it is, the fund is selling factor exposure at hedge fund prices.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 4, "What Goes Wrong", "What are the big risks in long/short equity?", "What goes wrong" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Five risks account for most long/short disasters. None of them is a secret, which is what makes them so instructive.</p>
    <h3>1. Crowding</h3>
    <p>Funds fish in the same pond: liquid mid-sized companies with a clear catalyst coming. So they often end up owning, and shorting, the same names. When something forces some of them to sell, prices move against everyone in the trade, losses trigger stop-losses, and the selling feeds itself. It happened in the August 2007 "quant quake" and again in the March 2020 COVID sell-off (Single-Manager vs Multi-Manager explains the mechanics).</p>
    <h3>2. Short squeezes</h3>
    <p>A heavily shorted stock that starts rising forces short sellers to buy back, which pushes it higher still. In January 2021, retail buyers coordinating online drove GameStop up by more than \(1{,}500\%\) in a few weeks. Melvin Capital, one of the best-known funds with a large short, lost about \(53\%\) that month, took a \(\$2.75\) billion rescue from Citadel and Point72, and wound down the following year. The Short Selling note has a squeeze interactive and the metrics funds use to spot crowded shorts.</p>
    <h3>3. Style and factor rotations</h3>
    <p>Many managers carry quiet style tilts. A fund full of fast-growing tech companies is partly a bet on "growth" beating "value". In 2020 and 2021, growth stocks soared; in 2022, as interest rates rose, the market rotated sharply to value and the Nasdaq fell about a third. Managers who'd looked brilliant on growth suddenly looked terrible, even where their individual picks were sound. Factor hedges (the last note's "Build the book") exist for exactly this.</p>
    <h3>4. Net exposure</h3>
    <p>A fund that's \(60\%\) net long loses roughly \(60\%\) of the market's fall in a crash, however good its stock picking. Net exposure feels like a dial the manager sets, but in a crash it decides most of the result.</p>
    <h3>5. The short side's open-ended loss</h3>
    <p>A long can lose at most everything. A short can lose many times over, because there's no ceiling on a share price. That's why short positions are kept smaller than longs, with strict limits and stops, and why the best short sellers spend as much time on risk as on research.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

