{% sec 6, "And in FX, Gold and Bonds?", "Is there alpha and beta in my three markets too?", "FX, gold and bonds" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>FX: carry is beta in disguise</h3>
    <p>In currencies, much of what looks like skill can be copied cheaply. Buying high-interest currencies against low-interest ones (carry), riding trends, and buying cheap currencies against expensive ones are all well-known "FX risk premia" that banks package into indices. A currency manager's true alpha is what's left after those. A trader who only made money in years when carry worked had beta, not edge.</p>
    <h3>Gold: miners are leveraged gold</h3>
    <p>Gold mining shares often move about one and a half to two times as much as the gold price, because a miner's costs are mostly fixed: a rise in gold drops almost straight into profit. So a fund that "picked a great miner" in a gold rally may simply have owned high gold beta. Pairing the miner against gold, as in the last note, shows whether the pick added anything.</p>
    <h3>Bonds: spread beta and the rebate's cousin</h3>
    <p>Most of a credit fund's return is the extra yield from taking credit risk (spread beta), which an investor can buy through a cheap bond ETF. A credit manager's alpha is choosing which borrowers to own and which to avoid. And the short rebate's logic applies to bonds too: funds that short bonds through repo earn interest on the cash, which rose sharply with rates in 2022 and 2023.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 7, "Key Words", "What does the desk call these things?" %}
  <div class="terms">
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Alpha</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Return from skill, after market and factors.</p><p class="eg">"Lots of beta, not much alpha."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Beta</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Return from simply being in the market.</p><p class="eg">"You're paying 2 and 20 for beta."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Rebate</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Interest earned on cash from short sales.</p><p class="eg">"At 4%, the rebate's a real line in the P&amp;L."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Squeeze</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Shorts forced to buy back, pushing the price higher.</p><p class="eg">"Cover before it squeezes."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Rotation</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Money moving from one style or sector to another.</p><p class="eg">"Brutal rotation into value today."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>De-gross</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Cutting both longs and shorts to reduce gross exposure.</p><p class="eg">"Everyone's de-grossing; crowded names are getting hit."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Hit rate</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The share of positions that make money.</p><p class="eg">"Hit rate's only 52%, but winners are twice the size of losers."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Up/down capture</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>How much of the market's rises and falls a fund catches.</p><p class="eg">"60% upside capture, 25% downside: that's what we want."</p></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 8, "How does this link to Trading, Real Estate and Fintech?", "Where alpha and beta turn up in each part of the plan.", "How it links to the plan" %}
  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Trading: measure your own beta</h3><a class="theme-link tone-forest" href="/hub/trading/">Trading</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>If you trade gold mostly from the long side and gold rises \(25\%\) in a year, a \(15\%\) return isn't evidence of skill: buying and holding would have beaten you. Compare your results with the simplest passive alternative for each market (holding gold, holding the currency with carry), and track your results in rising, falling and flat periods separately. Your edge is what survives that comparison.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Real estate: market beta versus adding value</h3><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A villa that rose \(30\%\) in value while local prices rose \(25\%\) made a \(5\%\) "alpha" from buying well or improving it; the rest was the market. Property investors who bought with heavy borrowing in a rising market often mistake beta and leverage for skill, until the market turns. Judging each project against a local price index keeps the record honest.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Fintech and Nigeria: beta in an inflationary market</h3><a class="theme-link tone-navy" href="/hub/fintech/">Fintech and Nigeria</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The NGX All-Share index rose about \(46\%\) in 2023 and nearly \(38\%\) in 2024, largely as investors moved out of naira cash into shares during high inflation and a falling currency. Almost any long-only fund looked good in naira. Measured against the market, and in dollars, many showed little alpha. Tools that show Nigerian investors their returns against the index and after inflation would make that obvious, and help them judge whether a manager earns its fee.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 9, "The Maths Underneath", "How do you split a return into alpha and beta?" %}
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">The regression allocators run</span>\[ r_{\text{fund},t} = \alpha + \beta\, r_{\text{mkt},t} + \varepsilon_t \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Beta from the data</span>\[ \beta = \frac{\operatorname{Cov}(r_{\text{fund}}, r_{\text{mkt}})}{\operatorname{Var}(r_{\text{mkt}})} \qquad \alpha = \bar r_{\text{fund}} - \beta\, \bar r_{\text{mkt}} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Peeling a year's return</span>\[ \alpha \approx r_{\text{fund}} - N_\beta\, r_{\text{mkt}} - S \times \text{rebate} - \text{factor tailwind} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>With monthly data, multiply the monthly alpha by \(12\) to get a yearly figure. \(R^2\) is the share of the fund's monthly ups and downs explained by the market: \(0.8\) means \(80\%\).</p>
  </div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example (Deltashark)</span>
    <h3>A fund made \(12\%\) in a year the market rose \(20\%\), at \(40\%\) net long. Now add that it was \(60\%\) short with a \(4\%\) rebate.</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Beta part: \(0.4 \times 20\% = 8\%\). Left: \(4\%\).</li>
      <li>Rebate: \(0.6 \times 4\% = 2.4\%\). Left: \(1.6\%\).</li>
      <li>If its growth tilt added another \(2\%\) in a growth-led year, the alpha was about \(-0.4\%\).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">A "good" \(12\%\) year can contain no skill at all once every layer is peeled off.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 10, "Practice", "Have a go, then open the answer." %}
  <div class="practice">
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">1</span><span>Explain alpha separation and why allocators care about it.</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Splitting a fund's return into alpha (skill) and beta (market exposure, which is cheap through index funds). Allocators pay high fees for alpha, so they strip out beta, and usually factors too, using regressions. A fund that made \(12\%\) with \(40\%\) net long in a \(20\%\) market year showed only about \(4\%\) of alpha.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">2</span><span>A manager claims to be market-neutral. List four questions you'd ask.</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Average net exposure; beta-adjusted net; factor exposures (value, momentum, size, sector); performance in the March 2020 sell-off and other crashes; use of leverage and behaviour when funds de-gross; how much of the return is explained by common factors.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">3</span><span>A \(\$300\) million fund is \(80\%\) short. Cash earns a \(4.5\%\) rebate. How much does the rebate add in a year, in dollars and as a return?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(0.8 \times 300 \times 4.5\% = \$10.8\) million, or \(3.6\%\) of the fund.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">4</span><span>A fund's regression against the market gives a beta of \(0.5\), a monthly alpha of \(0.3\%\) and an \(R^2\) of \(0.85\). What does each number tell you?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>It moves about \(0.5\%\) for each \(1\%\) the market moves; it makes about \(0.3\%\) a month (\(3.6\%\) a year) beyond that; and \(85\%\) of its monthly ups and downs are explained by the market, so most of its risk is market risk.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">5</span><span>Why can a fund with good stock picks still lose badly in a style rotation?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>If its longs share a style, such as growth, and its shorts share the opposite, the book carries a large factor bet. When money rotates from growth to value, as in 2022, that factor bet can swamp the stock-specific results.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">6</span><span>What happened to Melvin Capital, and what's the lesson for position sizing?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>A large short in GameStop was caught in the January 2021 squeeze; the fund lost about \(53\%\) that month, took a \(\$2.75\) billion rescue and wound down the next year. Lesson: a short's loss has no ceiling, so shorts must be smaller than longs, with hard limits, especially in heavily shorted, crowded names.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">7</span><span>Interview question: a fund returned \(15\%\) last year. Is that good?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>It depends on how it was earned. Ask what the market did, the fund's net and beta-adjusted exposure, its factor tilts, the short rebate, and how much risk it took (volatility, drawdown, Sharpe). \(15\%\) in a year the market rose \(30\%\), at \(70\%\) net long, is weak; \(15\%\) with low beta in a flat market is excellent. Then say you'd want several years to separate skill from luck.</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}
